# Tsumego mode, which until now could only be entered from the file chooser and
# so had no automated coverage whatsoever — not the bad-move path, not the
# Solved/Wrong feedback, not the blocked click on a solved position, not the
# resign refusal. `load_tsumego` is the file chooser's toggle exposed to scripts.
#
# tests/data/tsumego.sgf: a 9x9 corner problem, PL[B], solution B[bh] answered
# by W[ah]. Clicking takes two clicks in tsumego mode as everywhere else — one
# to take the stone out of the bowl, one to put it down.
#
# Coordinates: `click` takes board columns and rows, and board rows run opposite
# to SGF rows — board row N is SGF row (size-1-N). So the solution B[bh], SGF
# row 'h' = 7, is `click 1 1` on this 9x9, and the wrong move W[ah] would be
# `click 0 1`. Getting this backwards is silent: the click lands on an empty
# point, creates a branch, and the scenario fails somewhere further down.

load_tsumego tests/data/tsumego.sgf
wait_idle
expect tsumego true
expect board_size 9
expect view_position 0
expect main_line_moves 2
expect color_to_move B
expect phase paused

# A puzzle is not a game to concede, so Resign is refused — and the cursor is at
# the root with the solution ahead of it, which refuses it a second time over.
resign
wait_idle
expect has_result false
expect view_position 0

# --- A wrong move is marked, and says so ------------------------------------
# The point beside the solution is legal but wrong. It creates a branch off the
# correct tree, which the game thread infers as the first wrong move and tags BM.
click 0 1
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 0 1
wait_until msg tsumego_wrong
wait_idle
expect view_position 1
# The solution is untouched: a wrong move appends without promotion, so the
# main line still runs through B[bh]. This is what suppressSessionCopy and the
# non-promoted branch exist to protect.
expect main_line_moves 2

# Backing out of the dead branch clears the verdict.
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 0
expect msg none

# --- Going back into it must not promote it over the solution ---------------
# The wrong move is now an existing variation, so the click takes boardClick's
# *variation* branch rather than its new-move branch — and only the new-move
# branch carried `promote=false`. The second visit therefore promoted the wrong
# move to the main line and the recorded solution was displaced by it
# (main_line_moves 2 → 1). Both branches ask playVariationAt() now, which is the
# one place that knows a puzzle's answer is not a line to be overwritten.
click 0 1
wait_idle
click 0 1
wait_until msg tsumego_wrong
wait_idle
expect view_position 1
expect main_line_moves 2

# --- A dead branch can be explored, and the engine will punish it -----------
# Both halves of this were inert. GameSnapshot::onBadMovePath is published by
# navigateToVariation(), but markBadMove() runs *after* it returns — so the
# snapshot described the move one instant before it was condemned, and the two
# readers that ask about it both fail closed: no stone could be taken out of the
# bowl here, and Space never reached the engine. Nothing showed, because the
# "Wrong!" verdict is read from model.state rather than from the snapshot.
expect at_end true
click 4 4
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 4 4
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect msg tsumego_wrong
expect main_line_moves 2

# Space at the end of a dead branch asks the engine to refute it — the whole
# point of being allowed to play it out. Silence here read as "the key does
# nothing"; the log said nothing either, since the request was never queued.
expect can_kibitz true
key space
wait_idle
expect view_position 3
expect main_line_moves 2
expect phase paused

# The menu item is the same act and must reach the same engine. It did not: it
# took `play once`'s end-of-line branch, whose playKibitzMove() only hands the
# move to a player already blocked in genmove() and otherwise drops it into a
# slot nobody wakes for — so on a real configuration nothing was asked at all,
# while model.start() quietly turned the puzzle into a game. Against the mock it
# appeared to work, which is why only a run with real engines found it. Asserted
# through the command, since that is the half that was broken.
play once
wait_idle
expect view_position 4
expect main_line_moves 2
expect phase paused

navigate_start
wait_idle
expect view_position 0
expect msg none

# --- The solution plays the answer for you ----------------------------------
click 1 1
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 1 1
wait_until msg tsumego_solved
wait_idle
# Two moves deep: the solution, and the reply auto-played from the SGF.
expect view_position 2
expect at_end true

# --- A solved position is finished with ------------------------------------
# Clicking must not even pick a stone up, let alone record anything.
click 4 4
wait_idle
expect holds_stone false
expect view_position 2
expect msg tsumego_solved

# And neither may the Kibitz item, which is named "Hint" in Czech and was the
# one way left to play on past the answer: it appended the engine's move and
# started the game, at a position where the click above is refused outright.
# The button greys with the same answer now.
expect can_kibitz false
play once
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect phase paused

# --- Passing is a wrong answer, not a move that rewrites the problem --------
# `pass` is the same act as a board click at the cursor, and availableActions()
# says so — but the two agreed only as far as the snapshot they read. The pass
# command carried its own `model.start()` and its own default `promote = true`,
# so passing in a puzzle promoted the pass to the main line over the solution
# (main_line_moves 2 → 1) and flipped the phase to Playing. A puzzle is not a
# game to start; a pass in one is just another wrong answer.
navigate_start
wait_idle
expect can_pass true
pass
wait_until msg tsumego_wrong
wait_idle
expect phase paused
expect main_line_moves 2

# And the problem still is one: the recorded solution is still the solution.
navigate_start
wait_idle
click 1 1
wait_idle
click 1 1
wait_until msg tsumego_solved
wait_idle
expect at_end true

# Tsumego mode survives all of it, and leaves only by starting another game.
expect tsumego true
new_game 9
wait_idle
expect tsumego false
expect phase setup
